  "answer_text": "Hipparchus (c. 190\u2013120 BC) was an ancient Greek astronomer and mathematician, widely regarded as one of the greatest scientists of antiquity. He is often called the **\"Father of Astronomy\"**, a title conferred on him by Jean Baptiste Joseph Delambre in 1817. Here are his major inventions and contributions:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd0 1. Trigonometry\nHe made an early contribution to trigonometry by producing a **table of chords**, an early example of a trigonometric table; indeed, some historians go so far as to say that trigonometry was **invented by him**. The purpose of this table of chords was to give a method for solving triangles which avoided solving each triangle from first principles.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf0d 2. Precession of the Equinoxes\nHipparchus discovered the **precession of the equinoxes** by comparing his notes with earlier observers; his realization that the points of solstice and equinox moved slowly from east to west against the fixed background of the stars was one of his most significant achievements.\n\n---\n\n### \u2b50 3. First Star Catalog\nHe is credited with creating the **first reliable star catalog**, which included approximately 850 stars and established a system of stellar magnitude. Using a thin tube called a **dioptra** to scan the heavens, Hipparchus made extensive observations of star positions.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2d 4. The Astrolabe\nHis reputed achievements include the **possible invention of the astrolabe**, as well as of the **armillary sphere** that he may have used in creating the star catalogue. During his period of observations he may have invented the **planispheric astrolabe**, a device on which the celestial sphere is projected onto the plane of the equator.\n\n---\n\n### \u2600\ufe0f\ud83c\udf19 5. Models for the Sun and Moon\nHe was the first Greek to develop **quantitative and accurate models for the motion of the Sun and Moon**, making use of the observations and knowledge accumulated over centuries by the Chaldeans from Babylonia. Based on his solar and lunar theories and his numerical trigonometry, he was probably the first to develop a reliable method to **predict solar eclipses**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf10 6. Latitude and Longitude / Geographic Coordinates\nHe developed tools for measuring celestial angles and contributed to the systematic use of **geographic coordinates**, pioneering the application of **latitude and longitude**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcc5 7. Calculating the Length of the Year\nHipparchus **calculated the length of the year to within 6.5 minutes** and discovered the precession of the equinoxes.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udda5\ufe0f 8. Possible Inventor of the Antikythera Mechanism\nHe is one of the strongest contenders for the honor of being the inventor of the **Antikythera Mechanism** (also known as the Antikythera Device), considered the **world's first analogue computer**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd35 9. Division of the Circle into 360 Degrees\nHe also introduced the **division of a circle into 360 degrees** into Greece.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, Hipparchus's work laid the foundation for modern astronomy and mathematics, and his work laid important groundwork for future astronomers, including Ptolemy, whose own writings would dominate the field until the Renaissance.",
